  {"id":"chest-press","name":"Chest press","category":"Push","level":"Start here","description":"A supported press to learn a steady pushing movement.","primary":"Chest","secondary":["Shoulders","Triceps"],"color":"#e3e9d7","setup":"Adjust the seat so the handles line up with the middle of your chest. Keep your feet flat and your back against the pad.","steps":["Hold the handles with wrists straight.","Press forward without locking your elbows.","Return slowly until your elbows are just behind your body."],"feel":"You should feel your chest working, with help from the front of your shoulders and the backs of your arms.","common":"Keep your shoulders relaxed. Start with a weight you can move smoothly for every repetition.","video":null},
  {"id":"lat-pulldown","name":"Lat pulldown","category":"Pull","level":"Start here","description":"A guided pull that helps you learn to use your back.","primary":"Back","secondary":["Biceps","Shoulders"],"color":"#e3e8e6","setup":"Set the thigh pad snugly over your legs. Sit tall and take the bar a little wider than shoulder width.","steps":["Start with arms long and chest comfortably lifted.","Pull the bar toward your upper chest.","Let the bar travel upward slowly without shrugging."],"feel":"The sides of your back should do most of the work. Your arms assist the pull.","common":"Bring the bar in front of you, not behind your neck. Avoid swinging your torso.","video":null},
  {"id":"leg-press","name":"Leg press","category":"Legs","level":"Start here","description":"A stable way to practise a controlled lower-body push.","primary":"Legs","secondary":["Glutes","Calves"],"color":"#e9e5d7","setup":"Sit fully into the backrest. Place your feet around shoulder width on the platform and check the safety stops before starting.","steps":["Release the safety handles with the platform controlled.","Lower until your knees bend comfortably.","Push through the whole foot and stop before your knees lock."],"feel":"Your thighs and glutes should share the effort. Keep your lower back supported.","common":"Use a comfortable range. Do not let your knees collapse inward or bounce at the bottom.","video":null},
  {"id":"seated-row","name":"Seated cable row","category":"Pull","level":"Start here","description":"A seated pull for your back with room to focus on posture.","primary":"Back","secondary":["Biceps","Shoulders"],"color":"#e4e8de","setup":"Sit tall with feet on the supports and knees slightly bent. Hold the handle with arms extended.","steps":["Keep your chest easy and shoulders down.","Draw the handle toward your lower ribs.","Extend your arms slowly while staying tall."],"feel":"Think of your shoulder blades moving gently toward each other. Your arms will help.","common":"Avoid leaning far back or rounding your shoulders at the start.","video":null},
  {"id":"leg-curl","name":"Seated leg curl","category":"Legs","level":"Start here","description":"A simple machine for learning the back-of-leg movement.","primary":"Legs","secondary":["Glutes"],"color":"#e8e2d9","setup":"Align the machine pivot with your knee. Place the lower pad just above your heels and adjust the thigh pad to hold you comfortably.","steps":["Hold the handles and sit against the backrest.","Curl the pad down and back smoothly.","Return slowly without letting the weight stack crash."],"feel":"You should feel the backs of your thighs working through the motion.","common":"Keep your hips on the seat and use a light weight while you learn the setup.","video":null},
  {"id":"shoulder-press","name":"Shoulder press","category":"Push","level":"Start here","description":"A seated press to practise pushing overhead with support.","primary":"Shoulders","secondary":["Triceps","Chest"],"color":"#e4e6d9","setup":"Adjust the seat so the handles begin around shoulder height. Keep your back against the pad and feet planted.","steps":["Grip the handles without bending your wrists.","Press upward in a comfortable path.","Lower slowly until your hands return near shoulder level."],"feel":"The front and side of your shoulders help drive the press, with your triceps assisting.","common":"Stay in a pain-free range. Do not force the handles lower than feels comfortable.","video":null}
